**Basic Practice Problems**
Tables: `Employee(id, name, department_id)`, `Department(id, name)`

- J1. List each employee's name with their department name (INNER JOIN).
- J2. List every department along with employee names — include departments with zero employees (LEFT JOIN).
- J3. Find departments that currently have no employees (anti-join).
- J4. `Orders(order_id, customer_id)`, `Customers(id, name)` — find customers who have never placed an order.
- J5. Three tables: `Enrollments(student_id, course_id)`, `Students(id, name)`, `Courses(id, title)` — list student name + course title for all enrollments.


Answers

1.
select e.name ,d.name
from employee e
join department d
on e.department_id =d.id
;



2.
select d.name ,e.name
from department d
left join employee e
on e.department_id = d.id;


3

select d.name
from department d
left join employee e
on e.department_id =d.did
where e.department_id is null;



4
select c.name
from customer c
where c.id NOT IN
(select o.customer_id
from orders o
);


5.
select s.name,c.title
from students s
join enrollments e on
e.student_id=s.id
join courses c
on c.id = e.course_id
;
